1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,380
In the world, it became a legend before it even rolled off the production line.

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:08,360
Shrouded by a mantle of stealth, it can fly into harm's way,

3
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:12,820
deliver 40,000 pounds of destruction, and remain almost undetectable.

4
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,880
Now, the B-2 Stealth Bomber on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,860
On a cold spring morning on March 24, 1999, in the skies over Yugoslavia,

6
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:41,720
two B-2 stealth bombers are about to penetrate enemy airspace for the first time.

7
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:45,082
Early on, we weren't sure.

8
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:50,740
We weren't sure what the enemy was capable of seeing or anything like that.

9
00:00:50,741 --> 00:00:55,420
In fact, the first night of Operation Allied Force, as the airplanes are flying into Serbia,

10
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:59,780
there was a MiG engagement with an F-15 directly below the airplane.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
And it turns out the MiG had no idea that the B-2s were directly overhead.

12
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:12,000
And hearing that report just started to really quickly build the confidence in stealth technology.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
In the cockpits, the two-man crew is entering its 14th hour of continuous flying.

14
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:26,000
Having refueled over the Mediterranean Sea, they move into stealth mode

15
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:30,000
mode as the sleek shapes of the B-2s vanish into the night sky.

16
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:37,680
On the way there, you're not so much nervous as you are anticipating what you're about

17
00:01:37,681 --> 00:01:42,500
to go through, but you're also taking advantage of the time that you have to prepare yourself

18
00:01:42,501 --> 00:01:43,840
to do it well.

19
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:49,540
And so you spend time studying imagery and studying the targets and familiarizing yourself

20
00:01:49,541 --> 00:01:50,900
with where the threats might be.

21
00:01:50,901 --> 00:01:57,900
The two B-2s separate, one above the other, and climbing to combat altitude, begin their

22
00:01:57,901 --> 00:02:00,900
carefully choreographed bomb runs.

23
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:07,700
The B-2 pilots have no chaff, no flares, no high-speed afterburner, and no missiles to

24
00:02:07,701 --> 00:02:08,700
fire back.

25
00:02:08,701 --> 00:02:11,700
They only have one thing to protect them, their stealth.

26
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:16,900
Normally on a training mission, when we're just simulating the release of weapons, we

27
00:02:16,901 --> 00:02:18,900
don't open the weapons bay doors.

28
00:02:18,901 --> 00:02:23,440
Obviously on a combat mission, when you're dropping weapons out, the doors do come open.

29
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:26,880
And so there's drag on the airplane, there's noise on the airplane.

30
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,040
There's a clunk when the weapon falls off the airplane.

31
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:38,780
High over the target area, the aircraft shudders as weapons shoot out from its twin rotary launchers in the bomb bay of each B-2.

32
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:46,260
The most unique aspect of it, which really kind of gave me the sense of, hey, this is for real,

33
00:02:46,261 --> 00:02:50,000
is my first release, when the weapons started to come out

34
00:02:50,001 --> 00:02:54,720
and you felt the weapons dropping and being discharged from the airplane,

35
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:00,040
that's when the hairs on the back of my neck just started to kind of raise

36
00:03:00,041 --> 00:03:01,760
and I realized, hey, this is for real.

37
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:06,020
16 classified targets, 16 direct hits.

38
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,540
At the B-2's home base in Missouri,

39
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:15,200
anxious commanders scan the teletype machine

40
00:03:15,201 --> 00:03:16,820
for confirmation of the raid.

41
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:18,700
Suddenly there is a text.

42
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:21,860
Darth Zero One and Two, clean and green.

43
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:24,340
The B-2s are safe and heading for home.

44
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:32,280
In the final year of the 20th century,

45
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,700
the flying wing had come of age and gone into battle.

46
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,460
But nearly 100 years earlier,

47
00:03:39,461 --> 00:03:41,460
men had dreamt that the flying wing

48
00:03:41,461 --> 00:03:44,260
was the future of flight and the future of war.

49
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:57,159
But it wasn't until the early 1930s that the dream became a reality when two young brothers, Reimer and Walter Horton, first began to design wings for the new Nazi

50
00:03:57,160 --> 00:03:58,480
regime in Germany.

51
00:03:58,481 --> 00:04:03,979
The Horton brothers were very interested in highly efficient gliders.

52
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:14,260
And it's not such a great leap of imagination to realize that the most efficient form for any flying machine is just a pure wing.

53
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:24,960
With the outbreak of World War II in 1939, the brothers continue their groundbreaking work under a shroud of secrecy.

54
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:32,500
By 1941, they are exploring the use of a transport wing to help in the planned invasion of Britain.

55
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:40,300
At the same time, another German, Alexander Lippich, is working on his own wing design,

56
00:04:40,301 --> 00:04:47,920
when in late 1941, his ME-163 comet crosses the 600-mile-per-hour barrier for the first time.

57
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:54,520
The potential of rocket power combined with a flying wing design could change aviation history.

58
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,680
Meanwhile, the Horton brothers are going down another path.

59
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:04,600
By 1942, they are working on a 61-foot wingspan aircraft called the H-09.

60
00:05:04,601 --> 00:05:13,240
This would be powered by the Yunkers turbojet engines that were to be fitted to the ME262.

61
00:05:13,241 --> 00:05:20,560
The Herten No. 9 was the pinnacle of the brothers' development of the flying wing.

62
00:05:20,561 --> 00:05:25,960
And the prototype achieved a speed of something like 500 miles an hour.

63
00:05:25,961 --> 00:05:32,740
They had clearly demonstrated that you could build a vehicle with jet engines,

64
00:05:32,741 --> 00:05:36,800
that you could fly it, that it had very high speed potential,

65
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:40,680
and so they had really validated the concept.

66
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:47,820
Another of Reimer Horton's designs is the HO229.

67
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:51,260
Built of wood and with its engines housed on the wings

68
00:05:51,261 --> 00:05:53,400
with their exhausts vented on the top of the wing,

69
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:56,700
this aircraft would have been extremely difficult to detect.

70
00:05:57,320 --> 00:06:01,740
But if it could have flown, it would have had a top speed of 620 miles per hour,

71
00:06:01,741 --> 00:06:04,740
the fastest aircraft in World War II.

72
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:09,600
But while the Germans are developing their jets,

73
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:13,440
a 40-year-old American aircraft designer named Jack Northrup

74
00:06:13,441 --> 00:06:16,880
is quietly working on a flying wing design of his own.

75
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,240
Jack had been dreaming about flying wings since the 1920s

76
00:06:21,241 --> 00:06:24,840
and had long held the belief that the way to success in wing design

77
00:06:24,841 --> 00:06:28,600
was by reducing the drag created by a tail and fuselage.

78
00:06:28,601 --> 00:06:38,960
Northrop's idea was first of all to minimize the tail and the fuse at large and then as a last step to get rid of the tail altogether.

79
00:06:38,961 --> 00:06:46,180
So he went through a whole series of smaller steps in order to achieve the all wing configuration.

80
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:57,440
In December 1942, his NIM flying wing takes to the air and over the next few years, more and more of his designs are being refined and tested.

81
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:05,800
But by May 1945, the long and bitter struggle to defeat Nazi Germany is over.

82
00:07:05,801 --> 00:07:10,720
Its industrial and technological centers lie in ruin, along with its dream of a flying

83
00:07:10,721 --> 00:07:11,720
wing aircraft.

84
00:07:11,721 --> 00:07:17,220
But Jack Northrup is convinced that where the Germans failed, his wing will become the

85
00:07:17,221 --> 00:07:19,840
greatest aircraft in the world.

86
00:07:19,841 --> 00:07:25,520
All he would need is the raw power to do it.

87
00:07:25,521 --> 00:07:32,520
The Navy's Horton brothers were the first to utilize stealth technology with the invention of the H09 jet in 1942.

88
00:07:32,521 --> 00:07:39,520
The plane's wings were coated with a wood carbon powder composite that could absorb radar waves and escape detection.

89
00:07:39,521 --> 00:07:43,520
You are watching the B-2 Stealth Bomber on Modern Marvels.

90
00:07:43,521 --> 00:07:51,120
May 1945.

91
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:54,060
Nazi Germany is smashed into submission,

92
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:58,120
and with it are its hopes and dreams of building a flying wing aircraft.

93
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:06,700
But while post-war America continues to build conventional aircraft such as the B-29,

94
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,180
one man pursues his vision.

95
00:08:09,181 --> 00:08:17,020
In 1946, Jack Northrop tests his massive 400-mile-per-hour XB-35 flying wing.

96
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:23,320
Powered by four turboprop engines, this aircraft is seen by some as the bomber of the future.

97
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:30,080
This was where Northrop's vision really started to take shape,

98
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:36,780
because the XB-35 had a wingspan of about 60 meters.

99
00:08:36,781 --> 00:08:39,660
This is a big airplane, even by today's standards.

100
00:08:40,660 --> 00:08:45,800
Its problem was that whilst its top speed was about 400 miles an hour,

101
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:51,260
its speed in long-range cruise was only 185 miles an hour.

102
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:56,660
Just a year later, Northrop unleashes the new YB-49.

103
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:03,300
Powered by eight Allison jet engines,

104
00:09:03,301 --> 00:09:11,960
this 172-foot wingspan Goliath is capable of flying at over 500 miles per hour at a ceiling of 40,000 feet.

105
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:20,900
Jack Northrup has taken the American wing off the design boards and into the air.

106
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:27,260
Now, a preview of the flying wing transport of tomorrow.

107
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:31,900
Jack Northrup's vision for his flying wing is not just as a bomber.

108
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:34,720
He also sees it as a passenger aircraft.

109
00:09:35,220 --> 00:09:38,680
Because the wing could carry far greater loads than conventional aircraft,

110
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:43,440
his airliner would be capable of carrying 80 passengers with an observation lounge

111
00:09:43,441 --> 00:09:47,120
and an ability to fly coast to coast in four hours.

112
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:51,460
But the YB-49's days are numbered.

113
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:55,680
Although years ahead of its time, it is incredibly difficult to fly.

114
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:58,060
And without computers to help control it,

115
00:09:58,061 --> 00:10:02,660
the US Air Force feels that as a bomber it is too unstable and dangerous.

116
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:10,880
In late 1949, and in an unprecedented decision,

117
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:15,000
the government orders that all Northrop YB-49s are to be destroyed.

118
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:20,360
The flying wing looks destined to be dumped into the circular file of history.

119
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:25,620
From the Air Force's point of view,

120
00:10:25,621 --> 00:10:29,140
I don't think you could quibble with the decision they made on the day,

121
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:33,980
given the vehicles that were on the table in front of them.

122
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:40,360
But the destruction of the first flying wing comes at a perilous time in history.

123
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:45,480
Relationships between East and West are rapidly deteriorating,

124
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:47,500
and the Cold War is heating up.

125
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:52,760
The world enters a new era of nuclear power and nuclear testing.

126
00:10:55,620 --> 00:11:02,620
The Soviet Union has built up a huge arsenal of weapons, and the West responds.

127
00:11:02,621 --> 00:11:07,820
In a policy that is known as MAD, Mutually Assured Destruction, the heavy bomber is seen

128
00:11:07,821 --> 00:11:11,120
as the first line of attack.

129
00:11:11,121 --> 00:11:17,020
SAC, Strategic Air Command, puts into place a global system called Operation Chrome Dome,

130
00:11:17,021 --> 00:11:22,520
in which massive B-52s would constantly patrol the Earth, ready at a moment's notice to fly

131
00:11:22,521 --> 00:11:25,800
to any target deep within the Soviet Union and attack.

132
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:32,500
But SAC is also alarmed at the Soviet Union's new improved radar

133
00:11:32,501 --> 00:11:34,760
and SAM's surface-to-air missiles.

134
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:39,980
SAC is determined not to let its giant B-52 bomber fleet

135
00:11:39,981 --> 00:11:42,700
become a sitting target for the Soviet Union.

136
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:49,660
It became apparent that any bomber aircraft

137
00:11:49,661 --> 00:11:53,060
that had to penetrate Soviet airspace

138
00:11:53,061 --> 00:11:56,240
was going to have to run a layered gauntlet

139
00:11:56,241 --> 00:11:57,500
of air defense missiles

140
00:11:57,501 --> 00:11:59,460
that could shoot down aircraft

141
00:11:59,461 --> 00:12:01,980
no matter how high or how low they were flying.

142
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:05,700
The only way to evade Soviet air defense missiles

143
00:12:05,701 --> 00:12:08,120
was to fly so high

144
00:12:08,121 --> 00:12:11,040
that you're never going to hit a target with your bomb.

145
00:12:12,260 --> 00:12:14,620
What the U.S. needed was an aircraft

146
00:12:14,621 --> 00:12:16,600
that can evade the Soviet defense system,

147
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:19,320
fly to its target, deliver its weapons,

148
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:23,660
and bring its crew safely home, all under the cloak of invisibility.

149
00:12:23,661 --> 00:12:30,660
But to do this meant a completely new approach on designing and constructing aircraft.

150
00:12:30,661 --> 00:12:36,660
Every aircraft has what is called a radar cross-section.

151
00:12:36,661 --> 00:12:39,660
When an electromagnetic radar beam hits an aircraft,

152
00:12:39,661 --> 00:12:42,660
it bounces off anything that is large or pronounced,

153
00:12:42,661 --> 00:12:45,660
such as engines, straight wings, or cockpits.

154
00:12:45,661 --> 00:12:53,660
The radar operator sees all this information coming back and can determine the size, height, and direction of an aircraft.

155
00:12:53,661 --> 00:12:56,660
This is known as the aircraft's radar signature.

156
00:12:59,660 --> 00:13:05,660
But if you can hide this information from the enemy's radar, then the plane will become almost invisible.

157
00:13:05,661 --> 00:13:11,660
The mantle that will shroud the aircraft from its pursuers has a new name, stealth.

158
00:13:11,661 --> 00:13:14,293
During the mid-1970s, the U.S.

159
00:13:14,294 --> 00:13:19,560
government puts out a top-secret proposal to select aircraft

160
00:13:19,561 --> 00:13:21,740
manufacturers.

161
00:13:21,741 --> 00:13:26,380
Their objective is to come up with a feasible design for an advanced technology bomber,

162
00:13:26,381 --> 00:13:33,340
capable of long range, high payload, and low observability.

163
00:13:33,341 --> 00:13:36,660
By some strange irony, the winner is Northrop.

164
00:13:36,661 --> 00:13:43,180
Thirty years after seeing the destruction of their fleet of YB-49s, Northrop is back in the flying wing business.

165
00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:51,520
During the early 1980s, Northrop works on designs for their stealth bomber.

166
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:57,680
The entire project is buried deep within the highly classified and ultra-secret black program.

167
00:13:57,681 --> 00:13:59,111
At the time, the U.S.

168
00:13:59,112 --> 00:14:06,880
Black Program is so secret that even those that know about it are forbidden from ever acknowledging its existence.

169
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:16,300
Even Congress is denied from knowing how and where money is being spent.

170
00:14:18,040 --> 00:14:24,520
The Soviet Union was carefully examining things like American technical journals and the American defense budget

171
00:14:24,521 --> 00:14:28,700
so that they would know what threats they'd have to deal with in the upcoming years.

172
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:36,920
And in order not to have this very nifty new bit of stealth technology apparent to the Soviets

173
00:14:36,921 --> 00:14:41,260
just by picking up the annual published defense budget,

174
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:44,340
they had to make it what's called a black program.

175
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:51,800
With costs rising higher and higher, Northrop and its partners work against the clock.

176
00:14:52,620 --> 00:14:55,500
Originally, the contract had been clear in its requirement.

177
00:14:56,140 --> 00:14:59,320
Build a heavy bomber that could never be seen by enemy radar.

178
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:01,980
The solution seems impossible.

179
00:15:05,040 --> 00:15:09,540
What Northrop had to do was develop a computer system

180
00:15:09,541 --> 00:15:13,600
that would enable the B-2, as it came to be called,

181
00:15:13,601 --> 00:15:20,240
to stabilize itself using feedback and the fastest computers they could get.

182
00:15:20,241 --> 00:15:29,860
So the stabilization of a B-2 flying wing configuration becomes a software and computer hardware problem.

183
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:31,940
It becomes a cybernetic problem.

184
00:15:33,120 --> 00:15:36,300
By the mid-80s, and despite some of the most stringent checks,

185
00:15:36,780 --> 00:15:41,240
rumors began to circulate about what might be going on behind Jack Northrup's door.

186
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:47,900
Press reports, artists' impressions, magazine articles, and even automobile commercials

187
00:15:47,901 --> 00:15:52,900
begin guessing as to what is happening behind these doors.

188
00:15:56,900 --> 00:16:01,900
Finally, Northrop unveils its new aircraft on November 22, 1988,

189
00:16:01,901 --> 00:16:06,900
when it is rolled out of its hangar before an astonished crowd.

190
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:11,900
Called the B-2, the experts are staggered at this radical new aircraft.

191
00:16:11,901 --> 00:16:14,900
The crowds are only allowed to view it from the front,

192
00:16:14,901 --> 00:16:19,200
from the front, but they have never seen anything quite like it.

193
00:16:19,201 --> 00:16:24,200
Nobody could possibly keep this image off the television screen.

194
00:16:24,201 --> 00:16:26,140
Look at this thing.

195
00:16:26,141 --> 00:16:27,900
It's beautiful.

196
00:16:27,901 --> 00:16:28,760
It's odd.

197
00:16:28,761 --> 00:16:29,560
It's strange.

198
00:16:29,561 --> 00:16:30,360
It's exciting.

199
00:16:30,361 --> 00:16:33,260
And by God, it's American.

200
00:16:33,261 --> 00:16:35,580
But even as it rolls out of the hangar,

201
00:16:35,581 --> 00:16:37,660
time is running against the B-2.

202
00:16:41,140 --> 00:16:43,660
The Soviet Union seems like it is crumbling.

203
00:16:43,661 --> 00:16:46,060
and with it, the need for an intercontinental bomber.

204
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:49,680
The Cold War has come to a peaceful end.

205
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:54,500
It appears as though the need for a 21st century heavy stealth bomber

206
00:16:54,501 --> 00:16:57,160
might be consigned to the scrappy.

207
00:16:59,460 --> 00:17:02,440
Before starting his own aircraft company in 1938,

208
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:06,120
Jack Northrup assisted in the design of both the Douglas World Cruiser,

209
00:17:06,121 --> 00:17:08,160
the first plane to fly around the world,

210
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:12,440
and the Lockheed Vega, the plane Amelia Earhart flew nonstop across the Atlantic.

211
00:17:12,441 --> 00:17:19,440
modern marvels will be right back

212
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:24,520
on November 22nd 1988

213
00:17:24,521 --> 00:17:26,920
the Northrop corporation staggers the world

214
00:17:26,921 --> 00:17:32,160
when it unveils its B2 stealth bomber

215
00:17:32,161 --> 00:17:35,680
after years of working behind a complete screen of secrecy

216
00:17:35,681 --> 00:17:42,000
the team of visionaries at Northrop have rewritten the rules of aircraft design

217
00:17:42,001 --> 00:17:44,320
The technological array is awesome.

218
00:17:44,321 --> 00:17:48,960
With over 130 onboard computers controlling every element of the aircraft,

219
00:17:48,961 --> 00:17:52,040
the B-2 is light years ahead of anything yet built.

220
00:17:55,980 --> 00:18:01,680
A variety of techniques are combined to make this aircraft almost invisible to radar.

221
00:18:01,681 --> 00:18:07,740
Its smooth surface and shape consists of all curves, yet they are not consistently identical.

222
00:18:07,741 --> 00:18:11,820
the aircraft appears to continually change shape from whatever angle it is viewed,

223
00:18:12,340 --> 00:18:13,680
thereby confusing the radar,

224
00:18:14,180 --> 00:18:18,020
and whatever beams are locked onto it are either reflected into outer space

225
00:18:18,021 --> 00:18:20,380
or are reduced and dissipated into nothing.

226
00:18:22,980 --> 00:18:27,240
The B-2 is coated with special RAM, radar-absorbent materials,

227
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:31,340
such as carbon fiber composites and top-secret reflective paint

228
00:18:31,341 --> 00:18:33,980
that further reduces its detection from the enemy.

229
00:18:33,981 --> 00:18:41,440
Finally, its electronic countermeasures such as jamming an enemy's radar make this aircraft

230
00:18:41,441 --> 00:18:42,920
almost undetectable.

231
00:18:42,921 --> 00:18:50,100
In fact, the B-2's radar cross-section is 1,000 times less than that of the B-52.

232
00:18:50,101 --> 00:18:59,040
If you take a really big aircraft but make it essentially really flat, then when radar

233
00:18:59,041 --> 00:19:05,320
looks at it, instead of seeing a big round thing flying towards them, it'll just have

